 **Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-- **No Intersection:** If the ray does not intersect the surface within $[t_n, t_f]$ (i.e., no $s(x_i) \ge 0.5$), the method yields no fine samples. The implementation must handle this case, potentially falling back to standard sampling or discarding the ray [S2].
-- **Threshold Sensitivity:** The threshold of 0.5 is specific to the DoubleField formulation [S2]. Other SDF representations might use 0.0 (zero-level set). The implementer must ensure the threshold matches the normalization of the provided SDF field [S2].
-- **Precision:** The intersection detection relies on discrete coarse samples. If the surface is thin or the coarse sampling is too sparse, the intersection might be missed or inaccurately located. Increasing $N_s$ improves accuracy but increases cost [S2].
